UNIT escape_data
LANG ts
PRIMARY-SHA256 988966d9dcfe63af4de8d8707bc097ebd72c3b902496673ac2616c7d751524d6
SIG export function escapeData(v: unknown): string
ABI TypeScript compiles to the js runtime: text=string (UTF-16 code units); the result is a new string; R1 per code unit.
  STRICT: the artifact must type-check under `strict: true` against this SIG with no `any`, no non-null assertions (`!`),
  and no `@ts-ignore` / `@ts-expect-error`. The parameter is `unknown` because the runtime converts any value (ENVELOPE).
ENVELOPE widening (restated — language files are self-contained): any JS value is first converted with String(v), the
  generic ToString, NOT @actions/core's toCommandValue, then R1-R5 apply to that text. Numbers by JS Number-to-String
  ("NaN", "Infinity", "1e+21"; -0 -> "0"); bigint -> digits; booleans -> "true"/"false"; null -> "null"; undefined and a
  missing argument -> "undefined"; arrays -> elements joined with "," (null/undefined elements empty, nesting flattened);
  plain objects -> "[object Object]"; Map/Set -> "[object Map]"/"[object Set]"; RegExp -> "/source/flags"; Uint8Array ->
  decimal bytes joined by ","; Node Buffer -> UTF-8 decoded text (invalid bytes -> U+FFFD); boxed primitives -> their
  primitive's text. An object with a non-callable toString (directly or as an array element) -> throws TypeError
  "Cannot convert object to primitive value". Extra positional arguments are ignored. Text holding an unpaired surrogate half
  (not well-formed Unicode, so outside the primary) is in scope here and passes through unchanged (R4).
MECHANISM String(v), then .replace(/%/g, '%25').replace(/\r/g, '%0D').replace(/\n/g, '%0A') in that order (R2), or one
  pass with a function replacer over /[%\r\n]/g.
DEPENDS-ON none
PINNED regex ESCAPES = /[%\r\n]/g CITED R1 and TABLE ESCAPES — the same ECMAScript dialect as the js file
